  "worked_solution": "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we add a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always twice the real part of the complex number.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=3+5\\text{i},$ the real part is $Re(z)=3,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{\u2015}{z} & =2\\text{Re}(z) \\\\  & =2(3) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of adding the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=3+5\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{\u2015}{z}=3-5\\text{i}.$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{\u2015}{z} & =(3+5\\text{i})+(3-5\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(3+3)+\\text{i}(5-5) \\\\  & =6+\\text{i}0 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
